====Verb====
{{en-verb|~s|~ning|spun,span<l:uncommon>,~ned<l:nonstandard>|spun,~ned<l:nonstandard>}}

# {{lb|en|ergative}} To [[rotate]], [[revolve]], [[gyrate]] (usually quickly); to [[partially]] or [[completely]] rotate to [[face]] another [[direction]].
#: {{ux|en|I '''spun''' myself around a few times.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Spin''' the ball on the floor.}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''spun''' around and gave him a big smile.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1855|author=w:Henry Wadsworth Longfellow|chapter=Hiawatha’s Fasting|title=w:The Song of Hiawatha|location=Boston|publisher=Ticknor and Fields|page=76|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/hiawathasongof00longrich#page/76|passage=Round about him '''spun''' the landscape, / Sky and forest reeled together, / And his strong heart leaped within him, / As the sturgeon leaps and struggles / In a net to break its meshes.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1929|author=Agatha Christie|title=[[s:The Seven Dials Mystery/Chapter 17|The Seven Dials Mystery]]|text="Shall we '''spin''' for it?" "Might as well." "All right. Here goes. Heads you first and I second. Tails, vice versa." Bill nodded. The coin '''spun''' in the air. Jimmy bent to look at it.}}
## {{lb|en|aviation|of an aircraft}} To enter, or remain in, a [[#Noun|spin]] (abnormal stalled flight mode).
## {{lb|en|aviation|of a pilot}} To cause one's aircraft to enter or remain in a [[#Noun|spin]] (abnormal stalled flight mode).
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make [[yarn]] by twisting and winding fibers together.
#: {{ux|en|They '''spin''' the cotton into thread.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1718|author=w:Matthew Prior|chapter=Solomon on the Vanity of the World|title=Poems on Several Occasions|volume=II|location=Dublin|publisher=George Grierson|year_published=1738|section=book I|page=115|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=E4ZAByEHbQUC&pg=PA115|passage=Along the Sunny Bank, or Wat’ry Mead, / Ten thouſand Stalks their various Bloſſoms ſpread : / Peaceful and lowly in their native Soil, / They neither know to '''ſpin,''' nor care to toil ; / Yet with confeſs’d Magnificence deride / Our vile Attire, and Impotence of Pride.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Spin Off|year=2026|volume=L|number=1|page=42|title=Exploring S- and Z-Twist|author=Tom Knisley
|text=I started out by '''spinning''' a full bobbin of natural white cotton in the normal Z-spun direction.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[present]], [[describe]], or [[interpret]], or to introduce a [[bias]] or [[slant]], so as to give something a favorable or advantageous appearance.
#: {{syn|en|whitewash|sugarcoat|put lipstick on|gild|blandish|dress up}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1643|author=w:John Milton|title=Doctrine and Discipline of Divorce
|passage=But because he is but briefe, and these things of great consequence not to be kept obscure, I shall conceave it nothing above my duty either for the difficulty or the censure that may passe thereon, to communicate such thoughts as I also have had, and do offer them now in this generall labour of reformation, to the candid view both of Church and Magistrate; especially because I see it the hope of good men, that those irregular and unspirituall Courts have '''spun''' their utmost date in this Land; and some beter course must now be constituted.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=February 9, 2006|title=The Politics of Science|work=The Washington Post|url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2006/02/08/AR2006020801991.html|page=A22
|passage=In every administration there will be spokesmen and public affairs officers who try to '''spin''' the news to make the president look good. But this administration is trying to '''spin''' scientific data and muzzle scientists toward that end.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230530004017/https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2006/02/08/AR2006020801991.html|archivedate=30 May 2023}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Lisa Lerer; Nicholas Fandos|title=Already Distorting Jan. 6, G.O.P. Now Concocts Entire Counternarrative|work=The New York Times|date=2021-07-31|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/07/31/us/politics/jan-6-capitol-riot-pelosi.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=This past week{{...|, amid the emotional testimony of police officers at the first hearing of a House select committee,}} Republicans completed their journey through the looking-glass, '''spinning''' a new counternarrative of that deadly day.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260118072156/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/07/31/us/politics/jan-6-capitol-riot-pelosi.html|archivedate=18 January 2026}}
# {{lb|en|cricket|of a bowler}} To make the ball move sideways when it bounces on the pitch.
#: {{ux|en| This pitch is usually preferred for '''spin''' than pace.}}
# {{lb|en|cricket|of a ball}} To move sideways when bouncing.
#: {{ux|en| '''Spinning''' the ball gave him figures of 7-48.}}
# {{lb|en|cooking}} To form into thin strips or ribbons, as with sugar.
# To form (a web, a cocoon, silk, etc.) from threads produced by the extrusion of a viscid, transparent liquid, which hardens on coming into contact with the air; said of the spider, the silkworm, etc.
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2024|artist=w:Zeal & Ardor|album={{w|Greif (album)|Greif}}|title=Clawing Out|passage=I '''spin''' a thread that you can't see / A web of thoughts best left unclean
|note=Track 6}}
# To shape, as malleable sheet metal, into a hollow form, by bending or buckling it by pressing against it with a smooth hand tool or roller while the metal revolves, as in a lathe.
# To move swiftly.
#: {{ux|en|to '''spin''' along the road in a carriage, on a bicycle, etc.}}
# To stream or issue in a thread or a small current or jet.
#: {{ux|en|Blood '''spins''' from a vein.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|IV|ii|86|1|Mount them, and make inciſion in their Hides, / That their hot blood may '''ſpin''' in Engliſh eyes, / And [[dout|doubt]] them with ſuperfluous courage : ha.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|author=w:Jacob Geller|episode=Fear of Dark|date=15 Jan 25|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BcQ-8R2fiZg|time=2:52|passage=And yet, we refuse to simply perceive the dark. Rather than sit within total absence our brains '''spin''' imagined stimuli out into the void. After all, how could there truly be nothing there?}}
# {{lb|en|computing|programming|intransitive}} To [[wait]] in a [[loop]] until some [[condition]] becomes true.
# {{lb|en|motorsport|of a vehicle|intransitive}} To rotate into the gravel or managing to remain on the straight as a result of bad weather.
#: {{ux|en| His car '''spun''' in the rains just after finishing lap four.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To [[play]] ([[vinyl]] [[record]]s, etc.) as a [[disc jockey]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2002|journal=CMJ New Music Report|volume=70|issue=12
|passage=However, for the past six years he has been '''spinning''' his novel blend of progressive house and trance music and is finally on the brink of becoming the next luminary DJ.}}
# {{lb|en|cycling|intransitive}}
## To use an [[exercise bicycle]], especially as part of a gym class.
## To ride a [[bicycle]] at a fast [[cadence]].
# {{lb|en|UK|police|slang|transitive}} To [[search]] rapidly.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2013|author=Nick Oldham|title=Psycho Alley
|passage=But then again, unless someone struck lucky in those first few hours, there weren't even enough detectives to '''spin''' a [[drum]] [house].}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To draw out tediously; [[prolong]].
