====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|uncommon}} To [[feed]]: to provide with soup or a meal.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1845| title=Tales of the Colonies: Or, The Adventures of an Emigrant |author=Charles Rowcroft | page=432| passage=I'm blessed if I've heard about any thing but kangaroo-tail soup all the while I was at Launceston. They '''souped''' me there night and day.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1896| title=Readiana; Comments on Current Events |author=Charles Reade | page=2| passage=Now laughing together thaws our human ice; long before Swindon it was a talking match, —at Swindon who so devoted as Captain Dolignan,—he handed them out—he '''souped''' them,—he tough-chickened them,—he brandied and cochinealed one, and he brandied and burnt-sugared the other;}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1904|month=October|title=East is East and West is West|journal=The Vassar Miscellany|volume=34|issue=1|page=236
|passage="I was so mad, I let him wait half an hour to-night before I '''souped''' him."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Diza Sauers|title=Historama|page=152
|passage=She cooked huge stock pots and '''souped''' her dogs once a day.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=C Mark Chapoton|title=A Tale of Two Iditarods|page=34|passage=I '''souped''' the dogs, and went in for a bite. I ended up going back out and making my pups a full meal, then went back in and pigged out myself.}}
# {{lb|en|photography}} To [[develop]] (film) in a (chemical) developing [[solution]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1970|month=December|journal=The Rotarian|volume=117|number=6|page=31
|passage=That girl Vivienne, by the way, once worked as a secretary in the workshop of The Rotarian, began "'''souping'''" her own snapshots at home, went from there to top rank as a New York color photographer specializing in small children {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1991|author=Ruth Jean Dale|title=Society Page
|passage="Then perhaps it won't surprise you to learn Annie's taking over the Sunday social column," Roz said. "You photo-guys'll be '''souping''' her film."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1998|author=Edward Gorman|title=Cold Blue Midnight
|passage=And her camera position had been completely out of his sight. Satisfied that she'd gotten everything she'd needed - much more, in fact - she went back inside and got to work. Jill had '''souped''' her first photographs while she'd been on {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Jock Lauterer|title=Community Journalism: A Personal Approach|page=242|passage=By 6 pm Beau and I are back at the paper, '''souping''' the film, when Woody rushes into the room.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[proselytize]] by feeding the impoverished as long as they listen to one's preaching.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1855| title=A Digest of the Evidence, Taken Before the Select Committee of the House of Lords |author=William Le Poer Trench | page=280| passage=Was the priest who denounced those books of the National Board as "'''souping''' books" the patron of a national school?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1864| title=Irish Diamonds, Or, A Chronicle of Peterstown |author=Emily Bowles|page=203| passage="'''Souping'''" in Peterstown came to an end, and Una had enough to do with her full school and ignorant scholars to deaden the sting of her grief for the time. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1871| title=Hansard's Parliamentary Debates |author=Thomas Curson Hansard | page=1751| passage=It was suggested that the briefs should be distributed generally; but they could not be spread as they were at Manchester, Liverpool, and Leeds, where briefs where "'''souped'''" out.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1877| title=The Hon. Miss Ferrard|author= Mary Hartley | page=101| passage=Yes, and it was all done in pure charity, no '''souping''' swaddling mixture whatever. The Fitz-Ffoulkes had none of that about them .}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1887|month=January|page=36|work=Time: A Monthly Magazine |title=November in Kerry|author=|volume=5|number=|passage=Before long we passed a Scripture-reader (such the driver said he was), reading a book as a priest does his breviary. I though him not out of place; for anything madder than the whole system of "'''souping'''" it is hard to imagine. In Kerry you see signs of it here and there, as you do in Connemara.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1891| title=Hansard's Parliamentary Debates |author=Thomas Curson Hansard | page=838| passage=I ask, Sir, what right has the hon. Member to call any Protestant minister a '''souping''' parson?.}}

