===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|skeleton|zootomy}} A series of [[bone]]s situated at the [[back]] from the [[head]] to the [[pelvis]] of a human, or from the head to the [[tail]] of an animal, [[enclose|enclosing]] the [[spinal cord]] and providing support for the [[thorax]] and [[abdomen]].
#: {{syn|en|backbone|spinal column|vertebral column}}
#: {{hypo|en|C-spine|cervical spine|L-spine|railway spine|T-spine}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1851|author=w:Herman Melville|chapter=80|title=w:Moby-Dick|text=If you attentively regard almost any quadruped's '''spine''', you will be struck with the resemblance of its vertebrae to a strung necklace of dwarfed skulls.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=34|page=266|text=The preposterous altruism too! {{...}} Resist not evil. It is an insane immolation of self—as bad intrinsically as fakirs stabbing themselves or anchorites warping their '''spines''' in caves scarcely large enough for a fair-sized dog.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} [[courage|Courage]] or [[assertiveness]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Sydney I. Landau|title=Dictionaries: The Art and Craft of Lexicography|location=Cambridge|publisher=w:Cambridge University Press|page=409|isbn=978-0-521-78512-9|text=Trademark Owners will nevertheless try to dictate how their marks are to be represented, but dictionary publishers with '''spine''' can resist such pressure.}}
# Something resembling a [[backbone]], such as a [[ridge]], or a long, [[central]] structure from which other structures [[radiate]].
#: {{hypo|en|anterior superior iliac spine|dendritic spine|neural spine}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1838|author=Samuel Hare|chapter=4|title=Practical Observations on the Causes and Treatment of Curvatures of the Spine: With Hygienic Directions for the Physical Culture of Youth, as a Means of Preventing the Disease; an Etching and Description of an Apparatus for the Correction of the Deformity, and Engravings Illustrative of the Cases|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=fGoEAAAAQAAJ|location=London|publisher=Simpkin, Marshall & Co. [''et al.'']|section=Lateral Curvature|page=76|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=fGoEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA76|oclc=13279270|text=[Describing [[scoliosis]].] The integuments over the abdomen are folded or wrinkled, the left breast is seldom fully developed, the ribs lose their natural shape, those of the left side becoming straighter, while, on the right side, they are so much curved, as to admit of their being easily grasped by the hand; they are closer together on the left side, and frequently rest upon the '''spine''' of the ilium, thus giving the right side a fuller and more rounded appearance than is natural.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1994|author=Howard S. An|chapter=Anatomy of the Cervical Spine|coauthors=J. Michael Simpson|title=Surgery of the Cervical Spine|location=London|publisher=Martin Dunitz|section=Muscles and Fascia|page=14|isbn=978-1-85317-104-8|passage=The posterior muscles of the neck are divided into superficial, intermediate, and deep groups. The most superficial muscle is the trapezius, which originates from the external occipital protuberance and the medial nuchal line of C7 to T12 spinous processes and inserts onto the '''spine''' of the scapula, acromion, and the lateral aspect of the clavicle.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Peter Hyatt|chapter=Ian Moore Architects|title=Masters of Light: Designing the Luminous House|location=Mulgrave, Vic.|publisher=The Images Publishing Group|section=Air Apartments|page=242|isbn=978-1-86470-172-2|passage=The eastern tower and the smaller western satellite are linked by the lift core, which passes through the centre of the monorail turning circle and provides the structural '''spine''' of the building; the two towers are cantilevered from this '''spine'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Sergei Matveev|chapter=2 Complexity Theory of 3-Manifolds|title=Algorithmic Topology and Classification of 3-Manifolds|edition=2nd|location=Berlin|publisher=w:Springer Science+Business Media|section=2.3.2 Simplification Moves|page=75|isbn=978-3-540-45899-9|text=The polyhedron ''P''∪''D'' is a special '''spine''' of the twice punctured ''M'', that is, of ''M'' with two balls ''B''<sub>1</sub>, ''B''<sub>2</sub> cut out of it. To get a '''spine''' of ''M'', we make a hole in ''c''′ or ''c''″ depending on which of them is a common face of these balls.}}
# {{senseid|en|bound edge of book}} The [[narrow]], [[bound]] [[edge]] of a [[book]] that [[enclose]]s the inner edges of the [[page]]s, facing outwards when the book is on a [[shelf]] and typically [[bear]]ing the [[title]] and the [[author]]'s and [[publisher]]'s name.
#: {{syn|en|back<id:bound edge of book>}}
#* {{quote-web|en|year=1994–2014|title=Rare Book Basics: Book Terms Illustrated|archiveurl=http://web.archive.org/web/20140705063024/http://www.powells.com/rarebooks/bookbasics.html|archivedate=5 July 2014|work=[[w:Powell's Books|Powell's City of Books]]|text=The '''spine''' is the book's backbone. Because the '''spine''' is generally all you can see when a book is on the shelf, the spine displays the title and author of the book and is often ornately decorated.}}
# {{senseid|en|sharp}}{{lb|en|zootomy|botany|mycology}} A [[pointed]], fairly [[rigid]] [[protuberance]] or [[needlelike]] structure on an [[animal]], [[shell]], [[mushroom]] or [[plant]]. The botanical term technically refers to such a structure derived from a leaf or part of a leaf.
